NAME
       sudo, sudoedit - run a command as root (or another user) with your own password

SYNOPSIS
       sudo [-u USER] [-g GROUP] [-E] [-H] [-n] COMMAND
       sudo -i [-u USER] [COMMAND]
       sudo -s [-u USER] [COMMAND]
       sudo -l [-U USER]
       sudo -v | -k | -K
       sudo -e FILE...
       sudoedit FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       sudo runs a single command with the rights of another user, root by
       default. It asks for YOUR password (not root's), then checks the
       sudoers rules (/etc/sudoers and the files in /etc/sudoers.d) to decide
       whether you may run that command as that user. Every use is logged
       (in the journal and /var/log/secure), so the administrators know who
       did what.

       After a correct password, sudo remembers it for a few minutes (5 by
       default) on that terminal, so a series of commands asks only once.

       On RHEL 9 the members of the group wheel may run any command as any
       user (the rule "%wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL" in /etc/sudoers). To give a user
       full sudo rights, add the user to wheel: usermod -aG wheel USER. The
       user must log in again for the new group to count.

       A user who is not allowed is told so ("USER is not in the sudoers
       file" or "Sorry, user USER is not allowed to execute ..."), and the
       attempt is logged.

       sudoedit FILE (the same as sudo -e FILE) edits a file as root safely:
       sudo copies the file, opens the copy in your own editor as you, and
       copies it back when you save.

OPTIONS
       -u, --user=USER
              Run the command as USER instead of root.

       -g, --group=GROUP
              Run the command with GROUP as its primary group.

       -i, --login
              Start a login shell of the target user (root by default), as su -
              would, or run COMMAND through that login shell. sudo -i is how a
              wheel member becomes root without knowing root's password.

       -s, --shell
              Start a shell (from $SHELL) as the target user, keeping the
              current directory.

       -l, --list
              List what you (or with -U, another user) may run with sudo.

       -v, --validate
              Ask for the password now and extend the remembered time,
              without running a command.

       -k, --reset-timestamp
              Forget the remembered password, so the next sudo asks again.

       -K, --remove-timestamp
              Like -k, removing the remembered password completely.

       -n, --non-interactive
              Never ask for a password; fail if one would be needed. Good for
              scripts.

       -E, --preserve-env
              Keep your environment variables.

       -H, --set-home
              Set HOME to the target user's home directory.

       -e, --edit
              Edit files as root (sudoedit).

EXAMPLES
       Restart a service as student, who is in wheel:

           sudo systemctl restart httpd

       Become root with root's login environment:

           sudo -i

       Run a command as another user:

           sudo -u student touch /home/student/notes

       Show what the user student may run:

           sudo -l -U student

       Edit /etc/hosts safely:

           sudoedit /etc/hosts

FILES
       /etc/sudoers
              The main rules. Edit it only with visudo.

       /etc/sudoers.d/
              Extra rule files, read in name order. Create them with
              visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/NAME.

SEE ALSO
       sudoers(5), visudo(8), su(1), usermod(8)
